 ],
 "literal": "Grace and that which is not, what are they, if asked: non-killing and killing; value, that which is not, is eating that flesh.",
 "translation": "If asked what is grace and what is not, they are non-killing and killing. Eating that flesh is an act of no value.",
 "modern": "அருள் என்பது எது, அருள் அல்லாதது எது என்று கேட்டால், அவை முறையே ஓர் உயிரைக் கொல்லாமையும் கொல்லுதலும் ஆகும். அப்படிப் பெற்ற ஊனை உண்பது அறம் அல்லாத செயலாகும்.",
 "explanation": "The couplet defines grace and its opposite through the acts of non-killing and killing. It establishes that true compassion lies in not harming any life, while taking a life is the absence of compassion. Consequently, eating the flesh of a killed creature is condemned as an act devoid of any moral value or virtue. The word 'பொருள்' (poruḷ) here means a thing of value or virtue, emphasizing that eating meat brings no spiritual or moral worth.",
